<Item XName="Note" UserName="Note" Type="Notes">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Statistical Clutter Modeling Example&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies SystemVue&amp;nbsp;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;************************************************&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=4 face=Calibri&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Simulation Task&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;************************************************&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=3&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: left; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=left&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=3 face=Calibri&gt;This example demonstrates how to apply statistical distribution based clutter modeling for SystemVue radar library.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=3&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt; &#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;************************************************&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; &#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;FONT size=4&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=4 face=Calibri&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Workspace Description&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;************************************************&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;Clutter refers to radio frequency (RF) echoes returned from targets which are of no-interest to the radar operators. Generally clutter is caused by environmental factors such as sea, terrain, vegetation cover, etc. By traditional radar clutter theories, clutter could be modeled by several types of statistical models and add into the received echo.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;For&amp;nbsp;this workspace, the clutter modeling&amp;nbsp;methodology is proposed as: &lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0; -qt-paragraph-type: empty&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;1. A statistical model is set up to simulate the real world clutter.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0; -qt-paragraph-type: empty&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;2. The clutter has certain magnitude probability density functions and power spectrum densities.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;This workspace contains&amp;nbsp;two folders to demonstrate different aspects in statistical clutter modeling: &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;1, Clutter statistical distribution: &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;This example helps the user to understand how to generate statistical distribution based clutter using the ClutterGen part. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;The generated envelop clutter signal is transformed to complex baseband then gathered to see their corresponding statistical distribution functions. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;If the clutter amplitude follows&amp;nbsp;K-distribution, Design2 Analysis proposes the PDF with K distribution clutter data&amp;nbsp;generation.&amp;nbsp;The Ref_Dist_MATLAB equation&amp;nbsp;applies&amp;nbsp;3 methods to generate the K-distribution curves with identical results shown in &quot;PDF_K-RefCompare&quot; figure. The generated K-distributed clutter is shown in&amp;nbsp;&quot;PDF_K&quot; figure.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;If the clutter amplitude follows other distribution types, Sweep1&amp;nbsp;invokes Design4 Analysis to&amp;nbsp;demonstrate LogNormal, RayLeigh, Weibull for amplitude PDF data generation, and their PSD definitions are AllPole, Cauchy and Gauss based distributions, respectively. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;By equation file vars_graph (Automatic calculation&amp;nbsp;DISABLED), the clutter data&amp;nbsp;generation results&amp;nbsp;by Sweep1 are compared with standard PDF/PSD distribution&amp;nbsp;curves for each PDF/PSD type.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;2, Clutter generation with Targets&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;This example demonstrates how to add clutter impacts into multi-target detection scenarios.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;The traditional LFM source and transmitter with RF impariments are implemented, and three targets are designed in this example. Clutter modeling is performed with Radar_Clutter subnet, with Clutter_H and DelayEnv parts inside to simulate the clutter doppler frequency change and target echo delay effects (2*Range/C). The clutter power, clutter-to-signal power ratio modeling methods are shown in the equation of Design3 schematic.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;The three-target echoes and clutter&amp;nbsp;are added together then output to Sink and Spectrum Analyzer. If clutter is ENABLED (using s(1:4) for input port of AddEnv), then the target-2 echo amplitude is much higher than when clutter is DISABLED (using s(1:3) for input port of AddEnv).&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Note&lt;/STRONG&gt;: &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P style=&quot;MARGIN: 0px; TEXT-INDENT: 0px; -qt-block-indent: 0&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT face=Calibri&gt;For all the examples shown in this workspace, statistical clutter generation can only be applied with one specific set of range/doppler group. In other words, the generated clutter is ONLY applicable to ONE INTERESTED TARGET ONLY. For other targets or scatter groups, the clutter range/doppler must be redesigned and re-run simulation to see their impacts again. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;</Text>
